{"version":1,"summary":"Clean step-owned CI scratch and refuse destructive coverage paths.","files":[{"path":"scripts/ci","summary":"Clean owned scratch at step exit and validate caller coverage paths.","annotations":[{"newRange":[66,80],"summary":"The coverage guard compares directory identities before deleting stale coverage.","rationale":"Command substitution strips trailing newlines, so string comparison alone can validate a different directory. The -ef check refuses that mismatch. Eight guard cases and full scripts/ci pass on 0ba8ee0. Canary interrupts with orphaned word processes remain owned by the separate core signal-handling change.","author":"fm/verilex-ci-tmp-leak"}]},{"path":"scripts/ci-guards","summary":"Exercise unsafe coverage paths in scratch copies with sentinels.","annotations":[{"newRange":[1,56],"summary":"The guard test checks checkout, git and parent sentinels after each refusal.","rationale":"The find wrapper confines deletion to scratch, so the regression test can safely expose destructive old revisions. Extended cases fail on 89a36b5 and all eight pass on 0ba8ee0. A real caller coverage directory also retains tests.test and covmeta after scripts/ci test.","author":"fm/verilex-ci-tmp-leak"}]}]}
